'''Wheal Coates''' is a former [[tin]] mine situated on the north coast of [[Cornwall]], UK, on the cliff tops between [[Porthtowan]] and [[St Agnes, Cornwall|St Agnes]]. It is preserved and maintained by the [[National Trust]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/st-agnes-and-chapel-porth/ |title=St Agnes and Chapel Porth - Visitor information |publisher=National Trust |access-date=11 July 2015 |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150712194520/http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/st-agnes-and-chapel-porth/ |archive-date= Jul 12, 2015 }}</ref><ref name=cornwall/>

==History==
Earliest records indicate a mine at the site since 1692.<ref name=coates/>{{sfn|Kent|Kent|2008|p=63}} The present mine opened in 1802 and was closed in 1889 when the price of tin fell.{{sfn|Kent|Kent|2008|p=63}}<ref name=cornwall>{{cite web|url=https://www.cornwalls.co.uk/history/mines/wheal_coates.htm |title=Wheal Coates Mine - St Agnes (SW699500), Mining in Cornwall |publisher=Cornwall Guide |access-date=11 July 2015 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150712193639/https://www.cornwalls.co.uk/history/mines/wheal_coates.htm |archive-date= Jul 12, 2015 }}</ref> It came into full production in 1815.<ref name=coates>{{cite web|url=https://www.cornish-mining.org.uk/sites/default/files/imagebrowser/StAgnes_DD_v4.pdf|title=St Agnes: A diamond in Cornwall's mining crown|pages=1–6|publisher=Cornish Mining World Heritage|access-date=13 August 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305002557/https://www.cornish-mining.org.uk/sites/default/files/imagebrowser/StAgnes_DD_v4.pdf |archive-date= Mar 5, 2016 }}</ref> Flooding and bringing ore to the surface were the main problems of the mine until steam-driven equipment was available, as the mine's underground operations extended for some distance under the sea.{{sfn|Kent|Kent|2008|p=63}}{{sfn|''Mining and Scientific Press''|1906|p=736}}  The mine was sold in 1844 and thereafter allowed to flood. A new owner reopened the mine in 1872 but work was sporadic until its 1889 closure.{{sfn|Kent|Kent|2008|p=63}}<ref name=cornwall/> For some years, the yield was 20lb of tin per ton of ore.{{sfn|''Mining and Scientific Press''|1906|p=736}} In 1906, new ownership hoped to work the mine for both tin and copper.  Wheal Coates{{efn|'Wheal' is Cornish for 'place of work' or 'mine'.{{sfn|Jago|1882|p=[https://archive.org/details/ancientlanguaged00jago_1/page/308/mode/2up 309]}} Another term for 'mine' is 'bal', as in '[[bal maiden]]s', the women who worked on the surface.{{sfn|Jago|1882|p=[https://archive.org/details/ancientlanguaged00jago_1/page/110/mode/2up 111]}}}} had produced a small amount of copper ore, more than a century earlier.{{sfn|''Mining and Scientific Press''|1906|p=736}}<ref name=northern>{{cite web|url=http://www.nmrs.org.uk/mines-map/metal/cornwall-devon-mines/st-agnes-area/wheal-coates/|title=Wheal Coates|publisher=Northern Mine Research Society|access-date=13 August 2016 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160826090918/http://www.nmrs.org.uk/mines-map/metal/cornwall-devon-mines/st-agnes-area/wheal-coates/ |archive-date= Aug 26, 2016 }}</ref>

At the height of its production, 140 people were employed at the site<ref name=cornwall/> to mine a seam of tin just below sea level but this and a subsequent period of operation from 1911–1913{{sfn|''The Mining Magazine''|1911|p=274}}{{sfn|''The Mining Magazine''|1912|pp=261-262}} were not very successful because tin production was sporadic.{{sfn|Kent|Kent|2008|p=63}}{{efn|Some local people continue to believe there are rich tin ore deposits remaining in the area surrounding Wheal Coates.{{sfn|Kent|Kent|2008|p=63}}}} The mine was closed permanently in 1914.{{sfn|Ashley|2011|p=204}}

==Present day==

The surviving buildings date from the 1870s when deep underground mining began at the site<ref name=coates/> and were stabilised and preserved in 1986.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cornwallguideonline.co.uk/attractions/wheal-coates-tin-mine-st-agnes-porthtowan-1781.html|title=Wheal Coates Tin Mine|publisher=Cornwall Guide Online|access-date=13 August 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160905181303/http://www.cornwallguideonline.co.uk/attractions/wheal-coates-tin-mine-st-agnes-porthtowan-1781.html |archive-date= Sep 5, 2016 }}</ref> There are three engine houses that formerly housed [[Cornish engine]]s. Towanroath Pumping Engine House (1872) was used to pump water from the adjacent 600&nbsp;ft Towanroath shaft.{{sfn|Kent|Kent|2008|p=63}} There are two Whim engine houses which were used to crush ore for processing. "Old Whim" was built in the mid 19th century, while "New Whim" was built in the late 19th century.<ref name=Whim>{{cite web|url=https://www.historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1141460|title=Old Whim and New Whim|publisher=Historic England|access-date=13 August 2016}}</ref> A [[Calcination|calciner]] dating from 1910–1913 when the mine was reopened, roasted the tin to remove impurities such as [[arsenic]].<ref name=cornwall/>

The surviving structures were all listed as Grade II buildings on 31 October 1988: The Stamps House,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1138228|title=Stamps House at Wheal Coates|publisher=Historic England|access-date=13 August 2016}}</ref> the chimney east of the New Whim engine house,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1138270|title=Chimney Immediately East of New Whim|publisher=Historic England|access-date=13 August 2016}}</ref> the Old Whim and New Whim engine houses,<ref name=Whim/> the Towanroath engine house,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1141501|title=Towanroath Engine House|publisher=Historic England|access-date=13 August 2016}}</ref> and the calciner.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1328708|title=Calciner Immediately North of the STamps House|publisher=Historic England|access-date=13 August 2016}}</ref> Wheal Coates is part of the [[Cornwall and West Devon Mining Landscape]] [[World Heritage Site]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cornish-mining.org.uk/areas-places-activities/st-agnes-mining-district|title=St Agnes Mining District|publisher=Cornish Mining World Heritage|access-date=13 August 2016}}</ref>

