'''Allan Bank''' is a grade II [[Listed building|listed]] two-storey villa standing on high ground slightly to the west of [[Grasmere (village)|Grasmere]] village in the heart of the [[Lake District]].{{sfn|Gill|1989|p=272}}{{sfn|[Anon.]|2017}}  It is best known for being from 1808 to 1811 the home of [[William Wordsworth]], but it was also occupied at various times by [[Dorothy Wordsworth]], [[Dora Wordsworth]], [[Thomas De Quincey]], [[Samuel Taylor Coleridge]], [[Thomas Arnold]], [[Matthew Arnold]] and Canon [[Hardwicke Rawnsley]], a co-founder of The National Trust.  It is now owned by the [[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] and is open to the public.

==Construction==
Allan Bank is designed in a "bleakly Italianate" style according to [[The Buildings of England|Pevsner]],{{sfn|Hyde|Pevsner|2010|p=377}} faced with scored [[stucco]] and roofed with slate; it has been described as "large, though not handsome".{{sfn|[Anon.]|2017}}{{sfn|Schofield|2012|p=8}}{{sfn|Gill|1989|p=271}}  It was originally built by a [[Liverpool]] lawyer, John Gregory Crump, in 1805, and on its partial collapse the following year was rebuilt by him.{{sfn|Moorman|1968|pp=108–109}}{{sfn|Schofield|2012|p=8}}  An extension was added in 1834, perhaps designed by [[George Webster (architect)|George Webster]].{{sfn|Hyde|Pevsner|2010|p=377}}

==Wordsworth's tenancy==
[[Image:Henry_Eldridge_-_William_Wordsworth.jpg|thumb|[[William Wordsworth|Wordsworth]] in about 1807, when he took on Allan Bank]]
Initially [[William Wordsworth|Wordsworth]], who was then living less than a mile away in [[Dove Cottage]], was outraged by the building of Allan Bank.  In a letter to [[Richard Sharp (politician)|Richard Sharp]] he called it a "temple of abomination", and told him that "the house will stare you in the face from every part of the Vale [of Grasmere] and entirely destroy its character of simplicity and seclusion".  He soon had to overcome his objections however, since Dove Cottage was far too small for his growing family, and Allan Bank was the only large house in Grasmere he could rent.{{sfn|Moorman|1968|p=108}}<ref>{{cite book |last1=Johnston |first1=Kenneth R. |year=2000 |orig-year=1998 |title=The Hidden Wordsworth |location=London |publisher=Pimlico |page=585 |isbn=0712667520 }}</ref>  There he would have enough room for all his household, as well as guests, and his children would be able to play on the slopes of [[Silver How]] and the banks of [[Grasmere (lake)|Grasmere lake]].{{sfn|Gill|1989|p=271}}  He accordingly took up the tenancy in the summer of 1807 with the intention of moving in some time during the autumn, though in the event this was delayed until the end of May 1808.{{sfn|Pinion|1988|p=71}}{{sfn|Moorman|1968|p=133}}  "We already feel the comfort of having each a room of our own", wrote his sister [[Dorothy Wordsworth]] in June, but as the year drew on the Wordsworths began to change their minds as they realized that on windy days the various chimneys smoked appallingly.{{sfn|Moorman|1968|pp=133–134}}  Dorothy called the house "literally not habitable", and complained that "dishes are washed, and no sooner set in the pantry then they are covered with smoke".  On one stormy day, she wrote, "we could have no fire but in my Brother's Study – and that chimney smoked so much that we were obliged to go to bed with the Baby in the middle of the day to keep it warm, and I, with a candle in my hand, stumbled over a chair, unable to see it".{{sfn|Gill|1989|p=271}}  Workmen were periodically brought in to tackle the chimney problem but their many attempts did not produce a full solution.{{sfn|Moorman|1968|p=134}}  In the midst of these difficulties the Wordsworths entertained [[Thomas De Quincey]] on a visit that lasted for three months, and [[Samuel Taylor Coleridge]] moved in with the intention of making his permanent home with the Wordsworths, though after two fraught years he left for [[Robert Southey]]'s home in [[Keswick, Cumbria|Keswick]].{{sfn|Pinion|1988|pp=75–80}}{{sfn|Moorman|1968|p=187}}

By the beginning of 1810 they were looking for another house, and in May they decided to transfer themselves to the old parsonage in the centre of [[Grasmere (village)|Grasmere]] just as soon as it had been refurbished.{{sfn|Moorman|1968|p=156}}{{sfn|Pinion|1988|p=80}}  Dorothy immediately began to regret the impending loss of their wonderful views of Grasmere and Easedale, and declared the place "sweeter than paradise itself".<ref>{{cite book |last1=Levin |first1=Susan M. |year=2009 |orig-year=1987 |title=Dorothy Wordsworth and Romanticism |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Wva-MuFRhsEC&q=wordsworth+%22sweeter+than+paradise+itself%22&pg=PA59 |location=London |publisher=McFarland |page=59 |isbn=9780786441648 |access-date=7 June 2017 }}</ref>  After a further year had passed, and without the planned improvements to the parsonage having been made, they moved in June 1811, leaving Allan Bank to their landlord Mr. Crump.{{sfn|Pinion|1988|p=83}}  During the Allan Bank years Wordsworth had written ''The Convention of Cintra'', the first version of the ''[[Guide to the Lakes]]'' and most of ''[[The Excursion]]'', and revised ''[[The White Doe of Rylstone]]'', while Coleridge produced his journal ''The Friend''.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Smith |first1=K. E. |year=2015 |chapter=Wordsworth's Domestic Life, 1800–1850 |editor1-last=Gravil |editor1-first=Richard |editor2-last=Robinson |editor2-first=Daniel |title=The Oxford Handbook of William Wordsworth |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=nTTtBQAAQBAJ&q=%22much+more+spacious+Allan+Bank%22&pg=PA57 |location=Oxford |publisher=Oxford University Press |page=57 |isbn=9780199662128 |access-date=6 June 2017 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Rannie |first1=David Watson |year=1907 |title=Wordsworth and His Circle |url=https://archive.org/stream/wordsworthhiscir00rannuoft#page/149/mode/1up |location=London |publisher=Methuen |page=149 |access-date=7 June 2017 }}</ref>

==Later owners==
Thereafter Crump sometimes lived at Allan Bank himself, sometimes let it out to tenants, until it fell into other hands in 1831.{{sfn|Schofield|2012|pp=8–9}}  The educator and historian [[Thomas Arnold]] and his family spent the summer of 1833 there while their new house at nearby Fox How was being built;<ref>{{cite book |year=1911 |editor1-last=Knight |editor1-first=William |editor1-link=William Angus Knight |title=The Poetical Works of William Wordsworth, Volume 11 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=U74_AQAAMAAJ&q=%22their+future+home+at+Fox+How+was+building%22 |location=Edinburgh |publisher=W. Paterson |page=213 |access-date=7 June 2017 }}</ref> he worked on part of his ''History of Rome'' there, and boasted to a friend of the inspiring quality of the view from his window as he wrote.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Hamerton |first1=Philip Gilbert |author-link1=Philip Gilbert Hamerton |year=1875 |title=The Intellectual Life |url=https://archive.org/details/intellectuallif06hamegoog |edition=2nd |location=London |publisher=Macmillan |page=[https://archive.org/details/intellectuallif06hamegoog/page/n462 435] |access-date=7 June 2017 }}</ref>  One Thomas Dawson owned the house from 1834 till his death in 1894, sometimes taking short-term tenants, and it continued in his family until 1911.{{sfn|Schofield|2012|p=9}}  In 1915 it was bought by [[Hardwicke Rawnsley|Canon Rawnsley]], one of the co-founders of the [[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]], and he moved into it on his retirement in 1917.  He died there on 28 May 1920, leaving Allan Bank to the National Trust, though securing a lifetime's tenancy for his widow Eleanor Foster Simpson. She remained until her death in 1959.<ref>{{Cite ODNB|id=37884|title=Rawnsley, Hardwicke Drummond (1851–1920)}}</ref>{{sfn|Schofield|2012|p=9}}  In the 1950s a fire destroyed a large wing at the rear of the house, and a second fire in 2011 did further serious damage, Ending the 32 year tenancy of the Hunt Family, who threw some legendary parties in the 90's .  The National Trust thereupon began long-term restoration work, and in March 2012 opened Allan Bank to the public for the first time.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.theaa.com/days-out/allan-bank-and-grasmere-nt-7156641 |title=Allan Bank and Grasmere (NT) |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2015 |website=AA: Breakdown cover, insurance, route planner |publisher=AA plc |access-date=7 June 2017 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |title=National Trust to open fourth Wordsworth house in the Lake District |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/the-northerner/2011/nov/16/national-trust-williamwordsworth-allan-bank-rydal-mount-dove-cottage-cockermouth |work=The Guardian |date=16 November 2011 |access-date=7 June 2017 }}</ref>  In June 2017 they were describing the house as "still very much a work in progress".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/allan-bank-and-grasmere/features/the-house-at-allan-bank-and-grasmere |title=Indoors at Allan Bank |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2017 |website=National Trust |access-date=7 June 2017 }}</ref>

In October 2021, the building was one of 142 sites across England to receive part of a £35-million injection into the government's [[Culture Recovery Fund]].<ref>[https://historicengland.org.uk/whats-new/news/heritage-and-craft-workers-across-england-given-a-helping-hand/ "Heritage and Craft Workers Across England Given a Helping Hand"] – [[Historic England]], 22 October 2021</ref>

==Red squirrels==
Cumbria is one of the few remaining habitats in England of the [[red squirrel]],<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.forestry.gov.uk/forestry/INFD-5TPCW3 |title=Red squirrels |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=Forestry Commission England |access-date=2 October 2017 }}</ref> and the grounds of Allan Bank are known as a place where they are especially commonly seen.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rsne.org.uk/where-see-red-squirrels-0 |title=Where to see red squirrels |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2017 |website=Red Squirrels Northern England |access-date=2 October 2017 }}</ref>  They are encouraged by the National Trust staff there, who claim to spend £900 a year on feed, and by the Grasmere Red Squirrel Group.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/allan-bank-and-grasmere/features/sos-save-our-squirrels |title=Our native red squirrels |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=National Trust |access-date=2 October 2017 }}</ref>  There is a sculpture of a red squirrel in the gardens.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/p05hkz45/p05hkx78 |title=Red v grey squirrels |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2017 |website=Radio 4 You & Yours |publisher=BBC |access-date=2 October 2017 }}</ref>

